    "result_text": "Build succeeds and the test is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**Primary finding \u2014 agreed and fixed.** The `fiscalYearStart` duplication was real: I had copied the \"fiscal year starts 1 July\" rule into `ClinicServiceImpl` while `OwnerMapper` kept its own copy. I extracted it to a single authoritative home, `util/FiscalYear.startYear(LocalDate)`, alongside the other identity helpers (`LocalityResolver`, `LuhnCheckDigit`, `Sha256Hex`). Both layers now call it:\n- `ClinicServiceImpl.generateMemberId` (builds the FY segment into the memberId),\n- `OwnerMapper.formatFiscalYear` (the fallback path) **and** `OwnerMapper.formatMembershipPoints` \u2014 which was *also* encoding the same rule inline, so the extraction removed a third latent copy too.\n\nBoth private `fiscalYearStart` methods are gone; the boundary now lives in exactly one place.\n\n**Secondary note \u2014 left as-is, deliberately.** I did not centralize the memberId segment layout (the service building `<REGION><FY><HASH8><CHK>` vs. the mapper parsing it with the width `11`). As the reviewer noted, this is optional and follows the codebase's pre-existing pattern (the old code likewise parsed the identifier in the mapper, splitting `customerCode` on `'-'`). Introducing a shared build/parse `MemberId` type would be a larger structural change than this finding warrants, and the parse side only needs the invariant to read *existing* stored ids. Given it's explicitly marked optional and the risk/benefit is marginal here, I left it to avoid over-engineering \u2014 happy to do that extraction if you'd prefer the widths pinned in one spot.\n\nAcceptance behaviour is unchanged (`memberId=NSW2616E6609E3`, same as before), and the suite passes.",
